{
  "summary": "One finding on this computer needs your attention right away. A startup shortcut named OneDrive.lnk does not actually start OneDrive. Instead it runs PowerShell in a hidden window with a scrambled (encoded) command, which is a pattern malware often uses. Look at that startup entry first. Then confirm that you recognize the extra administrator account listed in the report.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Startup shortcut disguised as OneDrive runs a hidden, encoded PowerShell command",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:3b484bd3"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a shortcut called OneDrive.lnk in your personal Startup folder, so it runs every time you sign in. Its name suggests Microsoft OneDrive, but it actually launches PowerShell, a built-in Windows tool for running commands. It tells PowerShell to run with no visible window and passes it an encoded command, meaning the instructions are scrambled so you can't easily read them. Real OneDrive does not start this way. A hidden window, an encoded command and a familiar-looking name together are typical of software that is trying not to be noticed. The scan cannot tell what the command does, so treat this as serious until you know where it came from. The report shows the recommended fix for this finding. If you didn't set this up yourself, it would be wise to get help checking the computer more thoroughly."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize this administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The computer has a local administrator account shown as <computer>\\<account-2>. Administrator accounts can change anything on the computer. Having one is often normal; many people keep a separate admin account on purpose. If you or someone in your household created it, there is nothing to do. If you don't recognize it, take it more seriously, especially given the suspicious startup entry above. An unknown admin account can be a sign that someone else has gained access. The report shows what to do for this finding."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
